Saved 440 paired scenarios to hold_calibration_scenarios.csv

440 pairs across 11 decision gameweeks, 5-GW evaluation window. Within each pair both arms spend the same number of transfers over the same weeks and end with none banked -- only the timing differs.

REALISATION -- how much of each arm's predicted gain actually materialised:
Arm                       n     Mean predicted   Mean realised   Shrinkage   
spend now                 440   19.63            14.03           0.71        
bank, then bundle         440   27.99            14.73           0.53        

The hold shrinkage is the number the live tool needs: it is what a deferred gain computed from TODAY's model on a shifted window is actually worth once next week's information, churn and price drift have had their say.

HEAD TO HEAD (paired on squad and window, so most of the variance differences out):
Situation                         n     Spend     Hold      Hold edge   90% CI              Hold wins 
1 FT in hand, bank 1wk -> 2       110   9.92      12.78     +2.86       [+0.3, +5.4]        53        %
1 FT in hand, bank 2wk -> 3       110   13.90     13.29     -0.61       [-4.2, +3.0]        49        %
2 FT in hand, bank 1wk -> 3       110   14.66     14.76     +0.10       [-2.9, +3.0]        43        %
2 FT in hand, bank 2wk -> 4       110   17.65     18.09     +0.45       [-3.3, +4.1]        51        %

Overall hold edge: +0.70 pts (90% CI [-0.99, +2.24], holding won 49% of pairs)

By how good the SPEND-NOW option looked at decision time (this is the decision rule):
Spend-now predicted   n     Hold edge   90% CI              Hold wins 
6-10 pts              44    +0.84       [-3.5, +5.1]        41        %
10-20 pts             206   +0.84       [-1.5, +3.1]        53        %
20+ pts               180   +1.09       [-1.6, +3.7]        48        %

A positive hold edge means banking beat spending, on real points, over the same gameweeks with the same number of transfers. Read the CIs before believing any single row: these are noisy paired differences and the point estimates move around a lot.
